- net

' Identity as data. A name commits to a custody key. Custody signs a log of
' statements, each saying which key signs the name's pages from then on. A
' reader that holds a name's founding and its log knows the name's key by
' checking signatures, and asks no registry. These are the rules, pure: what
' a name is, what follows what, what a reader keeps on its card for a peer,
' and how a world that nobody has claimed yet comes to be someone's.
'
' Two keys, with different work. The live key signs pages and moments and
' lives where the world runs. The custody key signs only this log and is
' kept off the host: a host that is robbed, or dishonest, can speak for the
' name until custody says otherwise, and can never take the name.
+ pass
  + founding
    ' What a name commits to: the form of its identity, its first custody
    ' key, and a number its maker binds in with it (an invitation, the hash
    ' of its first tree, or nothing).
    : form=nat custody=nat dat=nat
  + form
    ' The one form there is: a single Ed25519 custody key, and a log of
    ' statements as this file reads them. A founding names its form and its
    ' name covers it, so another form (several custody keys, a signer of
    ' another kind) is another number and another name, never a second
    ' meaning for a name that exists.
    ^ nat
    1
  + name
    ' The name a founding makes: the low 128 bits of its hash, the width
    ' Mesa gives a name. A name therefore proves its own first custody key.
    \ made=pass/founding
    ^ nat
    = said=[f=nat c=nat d=nat] [made.form made.custody made.dat]
    | mod (PinHash (pin said)) 340282366920938463463374607431768211456
  + statement
    ' One entry of a name's log, signed by the custody key in force before
    ' it. seq numbers the entries from 1 and prev is the hash of the entry
    ' before (0 for the first), so a log is one chain. It states the key
    ' that signs from now (live), the life and rift that key speaks under,
    ' and the key that has custody from now.
    : seq=nat life=nat rift=nat live=nat custody=nat prev=nat sig=nat
  + word
    ' What a statement's signature is made over: the name and everything
    ' the statement says, as a plain row.
    \ name=nat seq=nat life=nat rift=nat live=nat custody=nat prev=nat
    ^ nat
    = said=[n=nat s=nat l=nat r=nat k=nat c=nat p=nat] [name seq life rift live custody prev]
    | PinHash (pin said)
  + link
    ' The hash the next statement chains to: this one, signature included.
    \ s=pass/statement
    ^ nat
    = said=[s=nat l=nat r=nat k=nat c=nat p=nat g=nat] [s.seq s.life s.rift s.live s.custody s.prev s.sig]
    | PinHash (pin said)
  + standing
    ' Where a name stands after its log: the number and hash of the last
    ' statement, and what it stated.
    : seq=nat last=nat life=nat rift=nat live=nat custody=nat
  + born
    ' A name before any statement: it has custody and no live key.
    \ made=pass/founding
    ^ pass/standing
    | pass/standing 0 0 0 0 0 made.custody
  + state
    ' Sign the statement that follows a standing, with the seed of the
    ' custody key in force.
    \ seed=nat name=nat at=pass/standing life=nat rift=nat live=nat custody=nat
    ^ pass/statement
    = seq at.seq.inc
      sig (Ed25519Sign seed (pass/word name seq life rift live custody at.last))
    | pass/statement seq life rift live custody at.last sig
  + next
    ' The standing a statement leaves, when it follows: the next number,
    ' chained to the last, signed by the custody in force. Nothing goes
    ' back, the first life is 1, and a new live key takes a new life, so a
    ' life names one key for ever.
    \ name=nat at=pass/standing s=pass/statement
    ^ either[iota pass/standing]
    ? !(eq s.seq at.seq.inc) (./err 'pass_gap)
    ? !(eq s.prev at.last) (./err 'pass_fork)
    ? !(eq 1 (Ed25519Verify at.custody (pass/word name s.seq s.life s.rift s.live s.custody s.prev) s.sig)) (./err 'pass_forged)
    ? (eq 0 s.life) (./err 'pass_back)
    ? (lt s.life at.life) (./err 'pass_back)
    ? (lt s.rift at.rift) (./err 'pass_back)
    ? (and (eq s.life at.life) !(eq s.live at.live)) (./err 'pass_key)
    | ./ok (pass/standing s.seq (pass/link s) s.life s.rift s.live s.custody)
  + read
    ' Where a name stands after a whole log, checked from its founding.
    \ name=nat made=pass/founding log=row[pass/statement]
    ^ either[iota pass/standing]
    ? !(eq name (pass/name made)) (./err 'pass_name)
    ? !(eq made.form pass/form) (./err 'pass_form)
    = start=either[iota pass/standing] (./ok (pass/born made))
    | log.fold start
      \ acc=either[iota pass/standing] s=pass/statement
      ^ either[iota pass/standing]
      ? acc
       > (./err why) acc
       > (./ok at) (pass/next name at s)
  + forked
    ' Two statements that both follow one standing and differ: proof that
    ' custody signed two futures. A reader keeps both and follows neither.
    \ name=nat at=pass/standing a=pass/statement b=pass/statement
    ^ bool
    ? (equal[pass/statement] a b) .false
    ? (either/is_err (pass/next name at a)) .false
    | either/is_ok (pass/next name at b)
  + lives
    ' Every live key a log has given its name, oldest first.
    \ log=row[pass/statement]
    ^ row[nat]
    = none=row[nat] []
    | log.fold none
      \ out=row[nat] s=pass/statement
      ^ row[nat]
      ? (out.any((\ seen=nat (eq seen s.live)))) out
      | out.snoc(s.live)
  + custody
    ' The custody seed an invitation's secret makes. Whoever holds the
    ' invitation holds custody; whoever only runs the world does not.
    \ secret=nat
    ^ nat
    | Sha256 secret
  + minted
    : name=nat made=pass/founding first=pass/statement
  + mint
    ' A name for a world nobody has claimed yet. Custody is the invitation's
    ' secret; the first statement gives the live key to whoever runs the
    ' world until it is claimed. The name is whole from this moment: others
    ' can address it, publish to it and cite it, and it will not change
    ' when it is claimed.
    \ secret=nat dat=nat live=nat
    ^ pass/minted
    = seed (pass/custody secret)
      made (pass/founding pass/form (Ed25519PublicKey seed) dat)
      name (pass/name made)
    | pass/minted name made (pass/state seed name (pass/born made) 1 0 live (Ed25519PublicKey seed))
  + claim
    ' Claiming is one statement, signed with the invitation's secret: the
    ' claimant's own custody key and a new live key, under the next life.
    ' Whoever ran the world before cannot make it, and after it their key
    ' signs nothing this name will be believed for.
    \ secret=nat name=nat at=pass/standing custody=nat live=nat
    ^ pass/statement
    | pass/state (pass/custody secret) name at at.life.inc at.rift live custody
  + record
    ' A statement as a record.
    \ s=pass/statement
    ^ myth
    . (myth/sing ['seq] (pails/n s.seq))
      put(['life] (pails/n s.life))
      put(['rift] (pails/n s.rift))
      put(['live] (pails/n s.live))
      put(['custody] (pails/n s.custody))
      put(['prev] (pails/n s.prev))
      put(['sig] (pails/n s.sig))
  + of
    \ record=myth
    ^ pass/statement
    | pass/statement record.nat_at(['seq] 0) record.nat_at(['life] 0) record.nat_at(['rift] 0) record.nat_at(['live] 0) record.nat_at(['custody] 0) record.nat_at(['prev] 0) record.nat_at(['sig] 0)
  + at
    ' Where a card keeps a name's log: its founding at <card>/pass and each
    ' statement beneath it by number.
    \ card=path seq=nat
    ^ path
    = base=path card.snoc('pass)
    ? (eq 0 seq) base
    | base.snoc(($u seq))
  + kept
    \ name=path state=namespace
    ^ maybe[myth]
    | (sovereign_care .x name state).fil
  + number
    ' The name a card is for: the number of the world it is kept under, or,
    ' for a world's own card (/<self>/io/mesa), the world itself.
    \ card=path
    ^ nat
    ? (lt card.len 3) 0
    ? (eq card.len 3) (quip_val card.at(0))
    | quip_val card.at(3)
  + held
    ' Where a card says its name stands; none while it holds no log.
    \ card=path state=namespace
    ^ maybe[pass/standing]
    = none=maybe[pass/standing] .none
    ?> (./some record) (pass/kept card state) none
    ? (eq 0 record.nat_at(['seq] 0)) none
    | ./some (pass/standing record.nat_at(['seq] 0) record.nat_at(['last] 0) record.nat_at(['life] 0) record.nat_at(['rift] 0) record.nat_at(['key] 0) record.nat_at(['custody] 0))
  + mark
    ' A standing as the slots a card keeps. key is the live key: the one
    ' landings are checked against (net/key).
    \ now=pass/standing
    ^ myth
    . (myth/sing ['seq] (pails/n now.seq))
      put(['last] (pails/n now.last))
      put(['life] (pails/n now.life))
      put(['rift] (pails/n now.rift))
      put(['key] (pails/n now.live))
      put(['custody] (pails/n now.custody))
  + meet
    ' First contact: keep a name's founding and log on the card for it. The
    ' log is checked against the name the card is kept under, so nothing an
    ' introducer says is taken on trust but the name itself.
    \ card=path made=pass/founding log=row[pass/statement] state=namespace
    ^ either[iota row[[p=path q=writ]]]
    | pass/meet_at card made log "" 0 state
  + meet_at
    ' The same for a name met through a scope that says where it is
    ' reached: the address is kept with the standing. The scope is taken
    ' at its word for the address, which gives reach, and for nothing
    ' else. With no port, no address is kept.
    \ card=path made=pass/founding log=row[pass/statement] host=str port=nat state=namespace
    ^ either[iota row[[p=path q=writ]]]
    ? (maybe/is_some (pass/held card state)) (./err 'pass_met)
    ? (eq 0 log.len) (./err 'pass_empty)
    ? (pass/read (pass/number card) made log)
     > (./err why) (./err why)
     > (./ok now)
      = born=myth (myth/sing ['custody] (pails/n made.custody)).put(['dat] (pails/n made.dat)).put(['form] (pails/n made.form))
        first=row[[p=path q=writ]] [[(pass/at card 0) (writ/make born)]]
        entries
          | log.fold first
            \ out=row[[p=path q=writ]] s=pass/statement
            ^ row[[p=path q=writ]]
            | out.snoc([(pass/at card s.seq) (writ/make (pass/record s))])
        mark=myth (pass/mark now)
        placed=myth (? (eq 0 port) mark mark.put(['host] (pails/t host)).put(['port] (pails/n port)))
        stand=writ
          ? (maybe/is_some (pass/kept card state)) (writ/poke placed)
          | writ/make placed
        head=row[[p=path q=writ]] [[card stand]]
      | ./ok head.weld(entries)
  + before
    ' Where the kept log stood before one of its statements.
    \ card=path seq=nat state=namespace
    ^ maybe[pass/standing]
    = none=maybe[pass/standing] .none
    ?> (./some born) (pass/kept (pass/at card 0) state) none
    = made (pass/founding born.nat_at(['form] 0) born.nat_at(['custody] 0) born.nat_at(['dat] 0))
      name (pass/number card)
    % k=nat 1
      at=pass/standing (pass/born made)
    ^ maybe[pass/standing]
    ? (ge k seq) (./some at)
    ?> (./some record) (pass/kept (pass/at card k) state) none
    ? (pass/next name at (pass/of record))
     > (./err why) none
     > (./ok now) (_ k.inc now)
  + turn
    ' A statement arrived for a name whose log is kept. One that follows
    ' is kept and the card moves to it. One already kept changes nothing.
    ' One that differs from what is kept at its number, and is custody's
    ' too, is a fork: it is kept beside the other as evidence, the card
    ' says so, and nothing is believed of the name until someone decides.
    ' Statements are their own proof, so it does not matter who brought it.
    \ card=path s=pass/statement state=namespace
    ^ either[iota row[[p=path q=writ]]]
    ?> (./some at) (pass/held card state) (./err 'pass_unmet)
    = name (pass/number card)
    ? (gt s.seq at.seq.inc) (./err 'pass_gap)
    ? (eq s.seq at.seq.inc)
      ? (pass/next name at s)
       > (./err why) (./err why)
       > (./ok now)
        | ./ok [[(pass/at card s.seq) (writ/make (pass/record s))] [card (writ/poke (pass/mark now))]]
    ?> (./some record) (pass/kept (pass/at card s.seq) state) (./err 'pass_known)
    = was (pass/of record)
    ? (equal[pass/statement] was s) (./err 'pass_known)
    ?> (./some then) (pass/before card s.seq state) (./err 'pass_known)
    ? !(pass/forked name then was s) (./err 'pass_forged)
    | ./ok [[(pass/at card s.seq).snoc('other) (writ/make (pass/record s))] [card (writ/poke (myth/sing ['fork] (pails/n s.seq)))]]
  + sent
    ' A statement as it travels: plain numbers, the same bytes from any
    ' kernel.
    \ s=pass/statement
    ^ nat
    = plain=[seq=nat life=nat rift=nat live=nat custody=nat prev=nat sig=nat] [s.seq s.life s.rift s.live s.custody s.prev s.sig]
    | PinBytes (pin plain)
  + got
    ' A statement from bytes that arrived; none unless they are one.
    \ bytes=nat
    ^ maybe[pass/statement]
    = none=maybe[pass/statement] .none
      stored=maybe[pin[any]] (PinFrom bytes)
    ?> (./some held) stored none
    ?> (./some plain) (fit[[seq=nat life=nat rift=nat live=nat custody=nat prev=nat sig=nat]] (unpin held)) none
    = [seq life rift live custody prev sig] plain
    | ./some (pass/statement seq life rift live custody prev sig)
  + introduction
    ' One world brought before another by a name that stands there already.
    ' to is the world it is shown to and by is the name that brings; name
    ' and made are the world brought and its founding; seq and last say how
    ' far its log had come; host and port are where it is reached. vouch is
    ' the signature of by's custody. consent is the signature of the custody
    ' of the world brought, over the same words: nobody is brought anywhere
    ' unless its own custody says so, and what is shown to one world proves
    ' nothing to another.
    : to=nat by=nat name=nat made=pass/founding seq=nat last=nat host=str port=nat vouch=nat consent=nat
  + brought
    ' What both signatures of an introduction are made over: ten numbers,
    ' where a statement's word is seven, so a signature made for one is
    ' never a signature of the other.
    \ to=nat by=nat name=nat made=pass/founding seq=nat last=nat host=str port=nat
    ^ nat
    = said=[t=nat b=nat n=nat f=nat c=nat d=nat s=nat l=nat h=nat p=nat] [to by name made.form made.custody made.dat seq last (str/to_atom host) port]
    | PinHash (pin said)
  + introduce
    ' Sign an introduction with the custody seed of the name that brings
    ' and the custody seed of the world brought, as that world's log stands.
    \ vouching=nat consenting=nat to=nat by=nat name=nat made=pass/founding at=pass/standing host=str port=nat
    ^ pass/introduction
    = words (pass/brought to by name made at.seq at.last host port)
    | pass/introduction to by name made at.seq at.last host port (Ed25519Sign vouching words) (Ed25519Sign consenting words)
  + introduced
    ' Whether an introduction holds for the world it was shown to. It is
    ' addressed to that world; the name is the one its founding makes; the
    ' custody of the name that brings signed it; and so did the custody the
    ' world brought had when its log stood where the words say. voucher is
    ' where the bringing name stands on the reader's own card for it, and
    ' log is the log of the world brought as far as the reader has it: one
    ' that has not come as far as the words say is short, and the reader
    ' waits for the rest. What comes back is where the world brought stands
    ' after the whole log.
    \ me=nat voucher=pass/standing log=row[pass/statement] i=pass/introduction
    ^ either[iota pass/standing]
    ? (pass/vouched me voucher i)
     > (./err why) (./err why)
     > (./ok words) (pass/consented log i words)
  + vouched
    ' The part of an introduction a reader checks before it holds any of
    ' the log of the world brought: whom it is for, the name, the form and
    ' the signature of the name that brings. What comes back is the words
    ' both signatures are over.
    \ me=nat voucher=pass/standing i=pass/introduction
    ^ either[iota nat]
    ? !(eq i.to me) (./err 'pass_other)
    ? !(eq i.name (pass/name i.made)) (./err 'pass_name)
    ? !(eq i.made.form pass/form) (./err 'pass_form)
    = words (pass/brought i.to i.by i.name i.made i.seq i.last i.host i.port)
    ? !(eq 1 (Ed25519Verify voucher.custody words i.vouch)) (./err 'pass_vouch)
    | ./ok words
  + consented
    ' The part that needs the log: the custody the world brought had when
    ' its log stood where the words say signed the words.
    \ log=row[pass/statement] i=pass/introduction words=nat
    ^ either[iota pass/standing]
    = start=either[iota pass/standing] (./ok (pass/born i.made))
      then
        | log.fold start
          \ acc=either[iota pass/standing] s=pass/statement
          ^ either[iota pass/standing]
          ? (gt s.seq i.seq) acc
          ? acc
           > (./err why) acc
           > (./ok at) (pass/next i.name at s)
    ?> (./ok at) then then
    ? !(and (eq at.seq i.seq) (eq at.last i.last)) (./err 'pass_short)
    ? !(eq 1 (Ed25519Verify at.custody words i.consent)) (./err 'pass_consent)
    | pass/read i.name i.made log
  + letter
    ' An introduction as it travels: plain numbers, the same bytes from any
    ' kernel. It is twelve numbers where a statement is seven, so neither
    ' is ever read as the other.
    \ i=pass/introduction
    ^ nat
    = plain=[to=nat by=nat name=nat form=nat custody=nat dat=nat seq=nat last=nat host=nat port=nat vouch=nat consent=nat] [i.to i.by i.name i.made.form i.made.custody i.made.dat i.seq i.last (str/to_atom i.host) i.port i.vouch i.consent]
    | PinBytes (pin plain)
  + opened
    ' An introduction from bytes that arrived; none unless they are one.
    \ bytes=nat
    ^ maybe[pass/introduction]
    = none=maybe[pass/introduction] .none
      stored=maybe[pin[any]] (PinFrom bytes)
    ?> (./some held) stored none
    ?> (./some plain) (fit[[to=nat by=nat name=nat form=nat custody=nat dat=nat seq=nat last=nat host=nat port=nat vouch=nat consent=nat]] (unpin held)) none
    = [to by name form custody dat seq last host port vouch consent] plain
    | ./some (pass/introduction to by name (pass/founding form custody dat) seq last (str/from_atom host) port vouch consent)
  + founded
    ' The founding a card keeps; none while it keeps none.
    \ card=path state=namespace
    ^ maybe[pass/founding]
    = none=maybe[pass/founding] .none
    ?> (./some born) (pass/kept (pass/at card 0) state) none
    | ./some (pass/founding born.nat_at(['form] 0) born.nat_at(['custody] 0) born.nat_at(['dat] 0))
  + beside
    ' The card kept for another name by the world that keeps this card.
    \ card=path name=nat
    ^ path
    [card.at(0) 'io 'mesa ($x name)]
  + log
    ' The statements a card keeps, in order, as far as it has followed.
    \ card=path state=namespace
    ^ row[pass/statement]
    = none=row[pass/statement] []
    ?> (./some at) (pass/held card state) none
    % k=nat 1
      out=row[pass/statement] none
    ^ row[pass/statement]
    ? (gt k at.seq) out
    ?> (./some record) (pass/kept (pass/at card k) state) out
    | _ k.inc out.snoc((pass/of record))
  + pending
    ' The introduction a card keeps for the world it is kept under.
    \ card=path state=namespace
    ^ maybe[pass/introduction]
    = none=maybe[pass/introduction] .none
    ?> (./some record) (pass/kept card state) none
    = word record.nat_at(['letter] 0)
    ? (eq 0 word) none
    | pass/opened word
  + welcome
    ' An introduction arrived for the world a card is kept under. It is
    ' taken when it is for this world, names the world its founding makes,
    ' and is vouched for by a name this world holds a log for: the world
    ' itself, when it brings itself (a minted name, claimed, saying where
    ' it now runs), or another name. It is then kept on the card as the
    ' record of who brought the world. It gives the card its address only
    ' when the log kept for the world reaches where the words say and that
    ' custody signed; until then the world is not heard. A card that has
    ' an address takes no other from an introduction: a world that moves
    ' says so by another rule.
    \ card=path i=pass/introduction state=namespace
    ^ either[iota row[[p=path q=writ]]]
    ? (lt card.len 4) (./err 'pass_name)
    ? !(eq i.name (pass/number card)) (./err 'pass_name)
    ? (eq 0 i.seq) (./err 'pass_empty)
    ? (gt (net/held ['port] card state) 0) (./err 'pass_met)
    = voucher=maybe[pass/standing]
        ? (eq i.by i.name) (pass/held card state)
        | pass/held (pass/beside card i.by) state
    ?> (./some stands) voucher (./err 'pass_unmet)
    ? (pass/vouched (quip_val card.at(0)) stands i)
     > (./err why) (./err why)
     > (./ok words) (pass/seat card i words state)
  + seat
    ' The writes welcome makes once an introduction is vouched for.
    \ card=path i=pass/introduction words=nat state=namespace
    ^ either[iota row[[p=path q=writ]]]
    = log (pass/log card state)
      kept=myth (myth/sing ['letter] (pails/n (pass/letter i)))
      route=myth kept.put(['host] (pails/t i.host)).put(['port] (pails/n i.port))
      keep=writ
        ? (maybe/is_some (pass/kept card state)) (writ/poke kept)
        | writ/make kept
    ? (lt log.len i.seq) (./ok [[card keep]])
    ? (pass/consented log i words)
     > (./err why) (./err why)
     > (./ok now) (./ok [[card (writ/poke route)]])
  + follow
    ' A statement arrived from a sender this world does not hear, for the
    ' card kept under that sender's name. Where the card has a log, the
    ' statement is taken as turn takes it. Where it has only an
    ' introduction, the first statement begins the log from the founding
    ' the introduction states, as meet does. A statement is its own proof
    ' and admits nobody: the address comes from the introduction alone.
    \ card=path s=pass/statement state=namespace
    ^ either[iota row[[p=path q=writ]]]
    ? (maybe/is_some (pass/held card state)) (pass/turn card s state)
    ?> (./some i) (pass/pending card state) (./err 'pass_unmet)
    = first=row[pass/statement] [s]
    | pass/meet card i.made first state
  + origin
    ' What a founding binds when its maker says what the world starts as:
    ' the pack its first tree is made from, at a version, and the world
    ' that made it, which the first tree lists as its maker. A world that
    ' starts as anything else is not the world the name was made for.
    : pack=path version=nat maker=nat
  + binds
    ' The number a founding carries for a first tree.
    \ f=pass/origin
    ^ nat
    = said=[p=path v=nat m=nat] [f.pack f.version f.maker]
    | PinHash (pin said)
  + begun
    ' Whether a founding is for this first tree.
    \ made=pass/founding f=pass/origin
    ^ bool
    | eq made.dat (pass/binds f)
  + grant
    ' Leave to act at a world's door: the hash of a session's secret and
    ' when it ends, signed by the custody of the world's name. It is
    ' checked against the custody in force when it is used, so a grant
    ' made before custody moved is nothing after: whoever held a world
    ' before its claim holds no door to it.
    : name=nat session=nat until=nat sig=nat
  + leave
    ' What a grant's signature is made over: four numbers, where a
    ' statement's word is seven and an introduction's ten.
    \ name=nat session=nat until=nat
    ^ nat
    = said=[d=nat n=nat s=nat u=nat] [1 name session until]
    | PinHash (pin said)
  + allow
    ' Sign a grant with the custody seed of the name.
    \ seed=nat name=nat session=nat until=nat
    ^ pass/grant
    | pass/grant name session until (Ed25519Sign seed (pass/leave name session until))
  + allowed
    ' Whether a grant opens this world's door now: it is for this world,
    ' it has not ended, and the custody in force signed it. What comes
    ' back is the session it is for.
    \ name=nat at=pass/standing now=nat g=pass/grant
    ^ either[iota nat]
    ? !(eq g.name name) (./err 'pass_other)
    ? (ge now g.until) (./err 'pass_ended)
    ? !(eq 1 (Ed25519Verify at.custody (pass/leave g.name g.session g.until) g.sig)) (./err 'pass_forged)
    | ./ok g.session
  + attested
    ' Whether a record, at the version its header states, is what a name
    ' said under any key its log has given it. A third world that was shown
    ' the record, its header and the log checks it without asking anyone.
    \ log=row[pass/statement] name=path said=oath record=myth
    ^ bool
    | (pass/lives log).any((\ key=nat (net/attested key name said record)))
